1
00:00:01,220 --> 00:00:02,750
Imagine the Moon vanished.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:03,440
Right now.

3
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:04,159
Gone.

4
00:00:05,129 --> 00:00:07,129
For the first one point three seconds,

5
00:00:07,179 --> 00:00:08,531
you wouldn't notice a thing.

6
00:00:08,961 --> 00:00:10,353
That's how long its last light

7
00:00:10,373 --> 00:00:11,801
takes to reach your eyes.

8
00:00:12,021 --> 00:00:13,414
Then the brightest thing in the

9
00:00:13,434 --> 00:00:15,133
night sky just switches off.

10
00:00:16,073 --> 00:00:20,223
Don't worry, the Moon isn't going anywhere.

11
00:00:20,243 --> 00:00:21,588
But this thought experiment is the best way

12
00:00:21,608 --> 00:00:23,088
to see what it actually does for us.

13
00:00:23,528 --> 00:00:25,746
And it does a lot more than you think.

14
00:00:25,766 --> 00:00:26,936
Our tides, our nights,

15
00:00:26,956 --> 00:00:28,412
a reef that waits for it,

16
00:00:28,432 --> 00:00:30,053
maybe even our seasons.

17
00:00:30,793 --> 00:00:32,816
So, what if the Moon disappeared?

18
00:00:38,546 --> 00:00:39,406
Chapter one.

19
00:00:39,426 --> 00:00:40,835
The night goes dark.

20
00:00:41,535 --> 00:00:42,715
Here's a surprise:

21
00:00:42,855 --> 00:00:45,554
the Moon is about as dark as worn asphalt.

22
00:00:45,984 --> 00:00:47,614
It reflects only around a tenth

23
00:00:47,634 --> 00:00:49,404
of the sunlight that hits it.

24
00:00:49,734 --> 00:00:51,373
It just looks bright because the

25
00:00:51,393 --> 00:00:52,945
sky around it is black.

26
00:00:53,375 --> 00:00:55,070
Even a full moon gives you about

27
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:56,755
a tenth of a lux of light.

28
00:00:57,075 --> 00:01:00,337
The Sun is about four hundred thousand times brighter.

29
00:01:00,767 --> 00:01:02,805
But that little bit of light matters.

30
00:01:02,825 --> 00:01:03,740
Without the Moon,

31
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,219
every night would be a new-moon night, forever.

32
00:01:06,239 --> 00:01:08,197
Just stars, and our own lamps.

33
00:01:08,627 --> 00:01:10,067
Astronomers would love it.

34
00:01:10,297 --> 00:01:12,602
Almost everyone else would need a flashlight.

35
00:01:13,062 --> 00:01:16,072
For us, that's an inconvenience.

36
00:01:16,092 --> 00:01:17,102
For some animals,

37
00:01:17,122 --> 00:01:17,476
moonlight is a map.

38
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:18,816
In Africa,

39
00:01:18,836 --> 00:01:20,658
dung beetles roll their dung balls

40
00:01:20,678 --> 00:01:22,366
in straight lines at night,

41
00:01:22,536 --> 00:01:26,016
by reading the pattern of polarized moonlight in the sky.

42
00:01:26,436 --> 00:01:28,985
They were the first animals ever shown to do that.

43
00:01:29,425 --> 00:01:30,825
And in the Arctic winter,

44
00:01:30,955 --> 00:01:32,915
when the Sun doesn't rise for months,

45
00:01:33,135 --> 00:01:35,588
tiny zooplankton across the ocean swim up

46
00:01:35,608 --> 00:01:37,741
and down by the Moon instead.

47
00:01:39,421 --> 00:01:40,281
Chapter two.

48
00:01:40,301 --> 00:01:41,497
The tides shrink.

49
00:01:42,187 --> 00:01:44,307
Tides aren't really the sea rising.

50
00:01:44,517 --> 00:01:46,621
They're the Moon stretching the whole planet.

51
00:01:47,051 --> 00:01:48,533
The Moon pulls a little harder on

52
00:01:48,553 --> 00:01:50,231
the side of Earth that faces it,

53
00:01:50,431 --> 00:01:52,391
and a little less on the far side.

54
00:01:52,641 --> 00:01:55,122
So the oceans bulge out on both sides.

55
00:01:55,562 --> 00:01:57,472
Earth spins under those bulges.

56
00:01:57,632 --> 00:02:00,359
That's why most coasts get two high tides a day.

57
00:02:00,809 --> 00:02:03,546
Not at exactly the same time each day, though.

58
00:02:03,566 --> 00:02:06,661
High tide comes about fifty minutes later every day,

59
00:02:06,681 --> 00:02:09,777
because the Moon keeps moving along its orbit.

60
00:02:10,217 --> 00:02:11,867
The Sun makes tides too.

61
00:02:12,137 --> 00:02:14,243
But it's so far away that its tidal pull

62
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:16,805
is only about half as strong as the Moon's.

63
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:18,335
So without the Moon,

64
00:02:18,355 --> 00:02:19,675
tides wouldn't stop.

65
00:02:19,935 --> 00:02:21,460
But the biggest tides would shrink to

66
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:23,066
about a third of their height.

67
00:02:23,496 --> 00:02:25,036
In Canada's Bay of Fundy,

68
00:02:25,136 --> 00:02:26,740
the water can rise and fall

69
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:28,586
by more than sixteen meters.

70
00:02:28,886 --> 00:02:30,777
That show would mostly be over.

71
00:02:31,217 --> 00:02:34,181
And the strip of shore between high and low tide,

72
00:02:34,201 --> 00:02:35,496
home to crabs, mussels,

73
00:02:35,516 --> 00:02:36,811
seaweed and shorebirds,

74
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:38,595
would get much narrower.

75
00:02:40,275 --> 00:02:41,185
Chapter three.

76
00:02:41,205 --> 00:02:42,991
The reef that waits for the Moon.

77
00:02:43,671 --> 00:02:45,061
On a few nights each year,

78
00:02:45,081 --> 00:02:47,571
a whole reef does something at the same moment.

79
00:02:47,791 --> 00:02:49,758
And part of the signal is the Moon.

80
00:02:50,188 --> 00:02:52,298
On Australia's Great Barrier Reef,

81
00:02:52,378 --> 00:02:54,098
many corals spawn together,

82
00:02:54,248 --> 00:02:56,948
one to six nights after a full moon in October,

83
00:02:57,008 --> 00:02:58,515
November or December.

84
00:02:58,975 --> 00:03:00,791
Billions of tiny bundles of eggs

85
00:03:00,811 --> 00:03:02,615
and sperm float up at once,

86
00:03:02,755 --> 00:03:04,605
like snow falling upwards.

87
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:07,525
Corals can't move to find each other.

88
00:03:07,635 --> 00:03:09,355
So timing is everything.

89
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:11,055
If they all let go together,

90
00:03:11,075 --> 00:03:12,908
the eggs have a chance to meet.

91
00:03:13,338 --> 00:03:15,138
Warm water sets the season.

92
00:03:15,258 --> 00:03:16,818
The Moon helps pick the night.

93
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:17,888
And right now,

94
00:03:17,908 --> 00:03:19,873
it's spawning season on the reef.

95
00:03:20,303 --> 00:03:21,263
Without the Moon,

96
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:23,463
the corals would lose one of their clocks,

97
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:26,304
and that perfect timing could start to fall apart.

98
00:03:27,984 --> 00:03:28,894
Chapter four.

99
00:03:28,914 --> 00:03:30,273
A wobbling planet?

100
00:03:30,953 --> 00:03:33,433
The Moon may be the reason we have steady seasons.

101
00:03:33,523 --> 00:03:34,663
Or maybe not.

102
00:03:34,883 --> 00:03:36,848
Scientists are still arguing.

103
00:03:37,278 --> 00:03:39,338
Earth spins on a tilted axis,

104
00:03:39,388 --> 00:03:41,288
about twenty three and a half degrees.

105
00:03:41,548 --> 00:03:44,047
That tilt is why we have summer and winter.

106
00:03:44,487 --> 00:03:46,177
Right now it barely moves.

107
00:03:46,397 --> 00:03:48,166
It rocks between about twenty two and

108
00:03:48,186 --> 00:03:49,907
twenty four and a half degrees,

109
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:51,887
over forty one thousand years.

110
00:03:52,317 --> 00:03:53,847
In 1993,

111
00:03:53,887 --> 00:03:55,404
a team led by Jacques Laskar

112
00:03:55,424 --> 00:03:57,387
ran the numbers without the Moon.

113
00:03:57,737 --> 00:03:59,357
Their answer was alarming.

114
00:03:59,697 --> 00:04:01,667
The tilt could swing chaotically,

115
00:04:01,687 --> 00:04:02,927
from nearly zero,

116
00:04:02,947 --> 00:04:05,017
up to about eighty five degrees.

117
00:04:05,857 --> 00:04:07,445
Eighty five degrees means a pole

118
00:04:07,465 --> 00:04:09,447
pointing almost straight at the Sun.

119
00:04:09,807 --> 00:04:11,257
Months of endless day,

120
00:04:11,297 --> 00:04:13,064
then months of endless night.

121
00:04:13,504 --> 00:04:15,044
But in 2012,

122
00:04:15,124 --> 00:04:16,924
another team took a closer look.

123
00:04:17,264 --> 00:04:18,514
On their moonless Earth,

124
00:04:18,654 --> 00:04:20,920
the tilt usually swung within a band only

125
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:23,384
about twenty to twenty five degrees wide,

126
00:04:23,554 --> 00:04:25,447
for hundreds of millions of years.

127
00:04:25,877 --> 00:04:26,655
So, wobblier?

128
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:26,939
Yes.

129
00:04:26,959 --> 00:04:27,281
Wild?

130
00:04:27,301 --> 00:04:27,964
Maybe not.

131
00:04:27,984 --> 00:04:29,672
The Moon is a stabilizer,

132
00:04:29,692 --> 00:04:33,116
but it's not the only thing keeping us steady.

133
00:04:34,796 --> 00:04:35,766
Chapter five.

134
00:04:35,786 --> 00:04:37,320
The brake on Earth's spin.

135
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,180
A day on Earth used to be much shorter.

136
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:41,610
And the Moon is a big

137
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:43,340
reason why it isn't anymore.

138
00:04:43,810 --> 00:04:46,910
Those tidal bulges don't line up perfectly with the Moon.

139
00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:48,790
Earth spins faster,

140
00:04:48,810 --> 00:04:50,582
so it drags them a little ahead.

141
00:04:51,012 --> 00:04:52,471
The Moon tugs them back,

142
00:04:52,491 --> 00:04:54,154
like a brake on the planet.

143
00:04:54,174 --> 00:04:56,459
Very slowly, the spin winds down.

144
00:04:56,889 --> 00:04:59,479
About six hundred and twenty million years ago,

145
00:04:59,559 --> 00:05:01,809
a day lasted under twenty two hours.

146
00:05:02,129 --> 00:05:04,199
One point four billion years ago,

147
00:05:04,219 --> 00:05:05,493
just over eighteen.

148
00:05:05,923 --> 00:05:08,617
Back then, a year had about four hundred days.

149
00:05:09,047 --> 00:05:10,767
Today the day still grows,

150
00:05:10,877 --> 00:05:13,747
by about two thousandths of a second every century.

151
00:05:14,197 --> 00:05:15,037
Without the Moon,

152
00:05:15,057 --> 00:05:16,797
the brake would mostly come off.

153
00:05:17,127 --> 00:05:19,827
The Sun's weaker tides would keep slowing us,

154
00:05:19,847 --> 00:05:21,181
but much more gently.

155
00:05:21,621 --> 00:05:22,881
And here's the twist.

156
00:05:23,101 --> 00:05:26,211
The spin that Earth loses goes into the Moon's orbit.

157
00:05:26,361 --> 00:05:28,914
That's why the Moon is slowly drifting away.

158
00:05:30,594 --> 00:05:31,544
Chapter six.

159
00:05:31,564 --> 00:05:32,883
The things we'd lose.

160
00:05:33,563 --> 00:05:36,323
The most perfect eclipse we know of happens right here.

161
00:05:36,473 --> 00:05:37,901
And it's a coincidence.

162
00:05:38,341 --> 00:05:39,814
The Sun is about four hundred

163
00:05:39,834 --> 00:05:41,371
times wider than the Moon,

164
00:05:41,501 --> 00:05:43,841
and about four hundred times farther away.

165
00:05:44,211 --> 00:05:45,271
So in our sky,

166
00:05:45,341 --> 00:05:47,873
they look almost exactly the same size.

167
00:05:48,303 --> 00:05:50,833
That's why the Moon can cover the Sun so neatly,

168
00:05:50,973 --> 00:05:53,217
and show us its glowing outer atmosphere.

169
00:05:53,637 --> 00:05:55,527
Eclipses changed science.

170
00:05:55,787 --> 00:05:58,907
In 1868, during an eclipse,

171
00:05:58,927 --> 00:06:00,909
Jules Janssen saw a strange yellow

172
00:06:00,929 --> 00:06:02,547
line in the Sun's light.

173
00:06:02,747 --> 00:06:03,947
It was a new element.

174
00:06:04,327 --> 00:06:06,617
Norman Lockyer named it helium,

175
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:08,480
after the Greek word for the Sun.

176
00:06:09,210 --> 00:06:10,780
In 1919,

177
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:15,070
another eclipse let astronomers measure starlight bending around the Sun.

178
00:06:15,490 --> 00:06:18,667
It was a test of Einstein's new theory of gravity.

179
00:06:19,107 --> 00:06:20,953
We'd lose our oldest clock, too.

180
00:06:20,973 --> 00:06:22,871
The word month comes from moon.

181
00:06:22,891 --> 00:06:25,152
The Islamic calendar still follows it,

182
00:06:25,172 --> 00:06:27,121
with years of three hundred and

183
00:06:27,141 --> 00:06:29,344
fifty four or fifty five days.

184
00:06:29,774 --> 00:06:31,874
And twelve people have walked on the Moon.

185
00:06:32,164 --> 00:06:33,314
In April this year,

186
00:06:33,334 --> 00:06:36,504
the four astronauts of Artemis two flew around it.

187
00:06:36,784 --> 00:06:39,189
Without it, there'd be nowhere close to go.

188
00:06:40,869 --> 00:06:41,859
Chapter seven.

189
00:06:41,879 --> 00:06:43,051
The real news.

190
00:06:43,731 --> 00:06:45,441
The Moon isn't going to vanish.

191
00:06:45,491 --> 00:06:46,661
But it is leaving.

192
00:06:47,101 --> 00:06:49,721
Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon.

193
00:06:50,101 --> 00:06:51,531
For more than fifty years,

194
00:06:51,551 --> 00:06:53,761
scientists have fired lasers at them,

195
00:06:53,861 --> 00:06:55,695
and timed the light's round trip.

196
00:06:56,125 --> 00:06:57,935
A pulse of light takes about two and

197
00:06:57,955 --> 00:07:00,163
a half seconds to get there and back.

198
00:07:00,583 --> 00:07:01,383
The result:

199
00:07:01,463 --> 00:07:03,002
the Moon moves away from us by

200
00:07:03,022 --> 00:07:05,323
about three point eight centimeters a year.

201
00:07:05,633 --> 00:07:08,152
Roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

202
00:07:08,582 --> 00:07:09,492
When it formed,

203
00:07:09,552 --> 00:07:12,802
after a Mars-sized world called Theia smashed into the young

204
00:07:12,822 --> 00:07:15,642
Earth about four and a half billion years ago,

205
00:07:15,922 --> 00:07:17,877
the Moon was maybe fifteen to twenty

206
00:07:17,897 --> 00:07:19,853
times closer than it is today.

207
00:07:20,293 --> 00:07:22,423
It has been backing away ever since.

208
00:07:22,753 --> 00:07:24,883
In roughly six hundred million years,

209
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:27,193
it will look too small to cover the Sun,

210
00:07:27,283 --> 00:07:30,209
and total solar eclipses will start to disappear.

211
00:07:30,649 --> 00:07:31,789
But it won't escape.

212
00:07:31,989 --> 00:07:33,079
Long before it could,

213
00:07:33,099 --> 00:07:34,827
the Sun itself will change.

214
00:07:35,867 --> 00:07:37,846
So, what if the Moon disappeared?

215
00:07:37,866 --> 00:07:39,056
We wouldn't all die.

216
00:07:39,076 --> 00:07:42,435
But we'd live on a darker, quieter, wobblier planet.

217
00:07:43,265 --> 00:07:44,495
Smaller tides.

218
00:07:44,555 --> 00:07:45,635
No eclipses.

219
00:07:45,825 --> 00:07:47,540
Animals missing a clock they've used

220
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:48,989
for a very long time.

221
00:07:49,409 --> 00:07:50,992
The Moon lights our nights,

222
00:07:51,012 --> 00:07:51,994
lifts our oceans,

223
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:53,446
helps steady our seasons,

224
00:07:53,466 --> 00:07:54,951
and slows our spin.

225
00:07:55,371 --> 00:07:56,981
The Moon doesn't just hang there.

226
00:07:57,181 --> 00:07:58,626
It has been shaping Earth since

227
00:07:58,646 --> 00:07:59,953
the day it was born.

228
00:08:00,903 --> 00:08:02,783
And there are two supermoons coming up,

229
00:08:02,803 --> 00:08:05,333
on November twenty fourth and on Christmas Eve.

230
00:08:05,683 --> 00:08:07,193
Go outside, look up,

231
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:08,356
and say thanks.

232
00:08:09,286 --> 00:08:11,036
If you enjoyed this,

233
00:08:11,056 --> 00:08:12,626
give it a like,

234
00:08:12,906 --> 00:08:14,286
and subscribe to Whyverse.

235
00:08:14,366 --> 00:08:16,056
And for shorter stories,

236
00:08:16,076 --> 00:08:16,531
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

